#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "112e3378-bde8-5896-822e-e815bde51501" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2878b05f-0cc8-5558-bbd3-257415b85a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386916} {:answer/id #uuid "7b439c2e-e085-544f-b9b9-d2813c513c1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386917} {:answer/id #uuid "7bed7f32-48ca-5123-8537-5e33396e49c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386918} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb3ab10-65c3-5081-944f-7ca4545f45ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}+3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386919} {:answer/id #uuid "f56ea238-3f2a-59dd-bfc5-349c182cd949" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b439c2e-e085-544f-b9b9-d2813c513c1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386917} :answer-field/id #uuid "d951c32d-2d00-5274-8524-bdbfc2ec5050" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b367e83d-4aeb-5069-8285-af1e0f742745" :question/math-academy-id "q-167453" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3}$ and $r(t)={t}^{3}\\,i+\\,j+t\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\,i+(2xy+{z}^{3})\\,j+3y{z}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{3},y=1,$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\,i+3{t}^{3}\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}i+(0)\\,j+\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\,i+3{t}^{3}\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}i+(0)\\,j+\\,k) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3{t}^{2}+3{t}^{3}\\cdot 0+3{t}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da1c5d4e-b495-5071-a38e-eb0a0b6b4a3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122255} {:answer/id #uuid "e35c0d1b-8d76-56c8-bc9f-05a9ea0766eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122256} {:answer/id #uuid "35c28916-89d0-57d1-94f5-ff29dbd00862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122257} {:answer/id #uuid "36ff272b-0c38-5d74-9a75-f15d64c7f980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122258} {:answer/id #uuid "40123f3b-09d3-560e-81ef-74fa8b78185c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35c28916-89d0-57d1-94f5-ff29dbd00862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122257} :answer-field/id #uuid "d747335a-d6e5-5823-901b-cd8c67c0ab13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ceb16fee-64a8-51ee-a3b7-7009e970259a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36215" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that\n$∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,j$\nand\n$r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2(t)}{(t)^{2}+3({t}^{2})}\\,i+\\frac{3}{(t)^{2}+3({t}^{2})}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+3{t}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}+3{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{4{t}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}}\\,j)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2t})\\cdot 1+(\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}})\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t}+\\frac{3}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\frac{2}{1}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157fafcf-277e-5966-a767-ecdea7988fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\pi +2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386923} {:answer/id #uuid "35f2a301-cf35-5997-b5ec-610c6fbc9394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386924} {:answer/id #uuid "76e71ef3-b781-5761-912b-4916a969ed98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386925} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8aa629-6db5-55fa-9c94-2b9b883d2021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386926} {:answer/id #uuid "12954625-2490-509e-9bb7-8410750430ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\pi +1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35f2a301-cf35-5997-b5ec-610c6fbc9394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386924} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c2ed97d-bde0-5593-89c6-6c05827a460f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "437b95b7-4f2c-5f75-b66d-dba87b082077" :question/math-academy-id "q-37106" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j$ and $r(t)=(2{t}^{2}+2)\\,i+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=2{t}^{2}+2$ and $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =4t\\,i+\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (4t\\,i+\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot 4t+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t+1}{({t}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\frac{2(1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1+1}{({1}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+1)}{(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi +2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf0a6486-b83a-50ef-b341-88f08ae4222a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e+{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386930} {:answer/id #uuid "3048ae93-6e5a-59cb-9fde-7f441569afb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386931} {:answer/id #uuid "41035f33-99ad-5b74-b0e7-21213b4a72fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386932} {:answer/id #uuid "9b666ede-136b-59ea-abb0-b14c3970227b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386933} {:answer/id #uuid "c411a985-c2d4-5747-b39a-ec7eb2c737a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-2{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c411a985-c2d4-5747-b39a-ec7eb2c737a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-2{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386934} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f2b8b13-d663-5198-9912-6a9983e28a54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "adf5d164-00ec-5671-9716-9934c5ad07b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-94873" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=2x\\,i+2y\\,j$ and $r(t)={e}^{-t}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2x\\,i+2y\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x={e}^{-t}$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2{e}^{-t}\\,i+2{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-t})\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{-t}\\,i+2{e}^{t}\\,j)\\cdot ((-{e}^{-t})\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-t}\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})+2{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =2{e}^{2(1)}-2{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}-2{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea1e5a55-a957-52cd-b693-0e48770672a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Rate of Change of a Multivariable Function at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34a34f72-bf20-5cfc-8b39-7e58bacb7f3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386937} {:answer/id #uuid "6524524c-7164-5995-a0a4-6096e3ddf8cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386938} {:answer/id #uuid "53a5ea5c-66ba-505f-b058-133057826fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386939} {:answer/id #uuid "3ed39ef7-08fb-5f16-a890-7cf4fd988318" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386940} {:answer/id #uuid "e2509dc9-b913-5304-a737-973bba047290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34a34f72-bf20-5cfc-8b39-7e58bacb7f3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386937} :answer-field/id #uuid "40c16ef5-25d5-5f1e-9dbd-2efa2010ad93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9259ded9-cd49-51af-9666-2b14f72ac749" :question/math-academy-id "q-94887" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\sqrt{t},3t-1⟩$ at the point $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t,y=\\sqrt{t}$ and $z=3t-1$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\sqrt{t}(3t-1),2t(3t-1),2t\\sqrt{t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(3t-1)\\sqrt{t},6{t}^{2}-2t,2t\\sqrt{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨(3t-1)\\sqrt{t},6{t}^{2}-2t,2t\\sqrt{t}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},3⟩ \\\\  & =(3t-1)\\sqrt{t}\\cdot 2+(6{t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+2t\\sqrt{t}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6t\\sqrt{t}-2\\sqrt{t}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}-t}{\\sqrt{t}}+6t\\sqrt{t} \\\\  & =12t\\sqrt{t}-2\\sqrt{t}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}-t}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =12(1)\\sqrt{1}-2\\sqrt{1}+\\frac{3(1)^{2}-1}{\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
